  "lineage": "Hawaiian fingerstyle guitar tradition that emerged after 1832 when Spanish-Mexican vaquero cowboys brought to Hawaii by King Kamehameha III to manage the new ranching industry left their guitars behind as gifts to Hawaiian musicians — who slackened the strings (kī hōʻalu literally means loosen the key) into open tunings and developed the fingerstyle vocabulary; 20th-century figures Gabby Pahinui (1921-1980, the voice, his Sons of Hawaii ensemble preserving the tradition), Sonny Chillingworth, Atta Isaacs, Raymond Kane, Leonard Kwan, Keola Beamer, the modern Cyril Pahinui and Ledward Kaapana lineage",
